Text fragments
description: a quoted selection - the selected text as the value, plus disambiguating context
- >
  A **text fragment** (prose, a markdown chunk) locates its region by **quotation, not by
  character offsets**. The member's **self-value is the selected text**, copied verbatim;
  `prefix` and `suffix` carry short runs of the text immediately before and after it:
- !!yo
  'reads the file twice: once to scan, once to build'
  yo: fragments:
    - once to scan
      type: text
      prefix: 'reads the file twice: '
      suffix: ', once to build'
- >
  In **marklower prose** the same member has an inline spelling - the [fragment
  token](*::documents:marklower:grammar): `[once to scan]()` written in place of the words is
  the **logical equivalent** of the explicit row above, and it is the *normalized* form there;
  the explicit row is the general spelling (and the only one in other formats). The token's
  position supplies `prefix`/`suffix` by itself, so an inline fragment never writes them.
- >
  To highlight the region again, the viewer searches the node's text for the quoted value;
  when the same words occur more than once, the surrounding context pins which occurrence is
  meant.
- >
  Character positions are **never the fragment's identity**: an edit earlier in the text would
  silently shift them onto the wrong words, while quotation survives edits elsewhere in the
  document - the fragment only breaks when the selected passage itself is rewritten, and then
  it breaks **visibly**, rather than highlighting the wrong place.
- >
  In a long text, though, positions earn their keep as an **accelerator**: optional `start` and
  `end` fields (in the example above, `once to scan` spans characters 22-34 of the chunk) let
  the viewer jump straight to the spot and pick the right occurrence among many repeats -
  the W3C practice of pairing its position selector with the quote. The quote stays the truth:
  when the text at `start`..`end` no longer matches it, the positions are stale - the viewer
  falls back to searching by quote, and may refresh or drop the fields on the next save.
- >
  The shape mirrors the W3C Web Annotation **TextQuoteSelector**, with its `exact` spelled as
  the member's self-value; the optional `start`/`end` pair mirrors its **TextPositionSelector**.
